PRACTICAL NO: 9
TITLE: INTERFACING RASPBERRY PI CAMERA MODULE TO CAPTURE IMAGE AND VIDEO

AIM:
To interface the official Raspberry Pi Camera Module with Raspberry Pi 4 using the Camera Serial Interface (CSI) port, configure the camera drivers, and write Python scripts to capture still photographs and record high-definition video clips.

COMPONENTS REQUIRED:
1. Raspberry Pi 4 Model B (or Pi 3) with Raspberry Pi OS
2. Raspberry Pi Camera Module (v1 / v2 / v3 / HQ)
3. 15-pin Ribbon Flat Flexible Cable (FFC)
4. Power Supply (5V 3A USB-C)
5. HDMI Monitor or remote desktop (VNC) for visual output

HARDWARE CONNECTIONS (CSI PORT):
1. Locate the CSI port on the Raspberry Pi board (between the HDMI port and 3.5mm audio jack).
2. Gently pull up the plastic locking tabs on the connector sides.
3. Insert the 15-pin ribbon cable:
   - Blue plastic stiffener faces towards the Ethernet / USB ports.
   - Exposed silver metal contacts face towards the micro-HDMI ports.
4. Press the plastic tabs back down to clamp the ribbon cable securely.
5. Insert the opposite end into the camera module PCB with contacts facing the camera lens circuit.

THEORY:

1. Camera Serial Interface (CSI-2):
The Raspberry Pi Camera connects via the dedicated CSI bus specified by the Mobile Industry Processor Interface (MIPI) Alliance. Unlike USB webcams that send frames over the shared USB bus and consume high CPU resources, the CSI bus transfers raw uncompressed pixel data directly to the Broadcom VideoCore Graphics Processing Unit (GPU) and Image Signal Processor (ISP) with low latency.

2. Software Architecture (Picamera2 vs Legacy Picamera):
- Legacy Stack (picamera): Relied on Broadcom's proprietary MMAL (Multi-Media Abstraction Layer) and OpenMAX firmware APIs.
- Modern Stack (Picamera2): Built on top of the open-source Linux libcamera framework and V4L2 (Video for Linux 2) kernel API, providing better color processing, auto-focus support (Camera Module 3), and broad compatibility across modern Debian distributions (Bullseye and Bookworm).

3. Sensor Warm-up and Auto-Tuning:
Image sensors require a brief settling period (typically 2 seconds of delay using time.sleep(2)) before grabbing a frame. This allows the internal automated algorithms—Auto Exposure (AE), Auto White Balance (AWB), and analogue gain control—to measure scene illumination and stabilize exposure levels.

4. Hardware Video Encoding:
The Raspberry Pi hardware video processing engine compresses raw frame buffers in real time using an onboard H.264 (MPEG-4 AVC) encoder, generating compressed video output files without overloading the main CPU cores.

CONSOLE SETUP COMMANDS:

Step 1: Check Camera Connection
Open terminal and verify sensor recognition:
rpicam-hello --list-cameras

Step 2: Install Libraries
sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3-pip python3-picamera2

(For older legacy OS using standard picamera):
sudo apt install python3-picamera

PYTHON SOURCE CODE:

Save file as camera_test.py

import time
from picamera2 import Picamera2

# Initialize the camera instance
picam2 = Picamera2()

# Configure still capture at 1920x1080 resolution
camera_config = picam2.create_still_configuration(main={"size": (1920, 1080)})
picam2.configure(camera_config)

# Start camera system
picam2.start()
print("Camera started. Warming up sensor...")
time.sleep(2)

# 1. Capture Still Photograph
image_filename = "photo.jpg"
picam2.capture_file(image_filename)
print("Photo saved successfully as", image_filename)

# 2. Record 5-Second Video
video_filename = "video.mp4"
print("Recording video for 5 seconds...")
picam2.start_recording(picam2.create_video_configuration(), video_filename)
time.sleep(5)
picam2.stop_recording()
print("Video saved successfully as", video_filename)

# Release camera hardware cleanly
picam2.stop()
picam2.close()
print("Camera released safely.")

